          "title": "1.1 Definition of Literature, Oral Tradition vs. Written Tradition",
          "image": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/7334191/pexels-photo-7334191.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
          "video": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lVv98LDQEDs",
          "content": "Definition of Literature:\nLiterature refers to writings considered to be of artistic or intellectual value, characterized by creative imagination, aesthetic qualities, and the skilled use of language. It typically encompasses works of fiction and non-fiction that are notable for their form, style, and ability to evoke thought and emotion.\n\nWhile the term can broadly include any written material, in academic study, \"literature\" usually refers to works such as poetry, drama, novels, short stories, and essays that offer insights into human nature, society, and the world, often through artistic expression.\n\nKey characteristics often associated with literary works:\n\nImaginative/Creative: Often involves fictional narratives, characters, or unique perspectives.\n\nArtistic Merit: Demonstrates skill in the use of language, form, and technique.\n\nUniversal Themes: Explores timeless human experiences, emotions, and ideas.\n\nEmotional Impact: Aims to evoke feelings and responses in the reader.\n\nIntellectual Value: Encourages thought, reflection, and interpretation.\n\nEnduring Quality: Often remains relevant and appreciated across different time periods and cultures.\n\nOral Tradition vs. Written Tradition:\nLiterature has roots in both oral and written forms of expression.\n\nOral Tradition (Orature):\n\nRefers to the body of literary works (stories, myths, legends, epics, poems, songs, proverbs, riddles) that are composed, transmitted, and preserved through spoken word rather than writing.\n\nIt is the oldest form of literature, predating the invention of writing systems.\n\nCharacteristics:\n\nPerformance-based: Often involves storytelling, recitation, singing, or dramatic enactment, with elements like gestures, voice modulation, and audience interaction being important.\n\nCommunal Ownership: Stories and songs often belong to the community rather than a single identifiable author.\n\nVariability: As works are passed down orally from generation to generation, they can undergo changes, adaptations, and variations.\n\nMemory and Mnemonic Devices: Relies on memory; often uses repetition, rhythm, rhyme, stock phrases, and formulaic structures to aid memorization and transmission.\n\nSocial Function: Plays a crucial role in transmitting cultural values, history, beliefs, moral lessons, and entertainment within a community.\n\nExamples: African folktales, epic poems like Homer's Iliad and Odyssey (which originated orally), traditional ballads, proverbs, myths and legends from various cultures.\n\nMany written literary works draw inspiration from, or incorporate elements of, oral traditions.\n\nWritten Tradition:\n\nRefers to literary works that are composed, transmitted, and preserved in written form using an alphabet or other writing system.\n\nThe invention of writing allowed for:\n\nFixity of Text: Greater stability and permanence of the literary work compared to oral versions.\n\nIndividual Authorship: Clearer identification of the author.\n\nWider Dissemination: Potentially reaching a larger and more dispersed audience over time and space (especially after invention of printing).\n\nDevelopment of Complex Forms: Written form facilitates more intricate plots, detailed characterization, and complex linguistic structures that might be harder to sustain in purely oral forms.\n\nThe shift from predominantly oral to written traditions was a gradual process, and both traditions can coexist and influence each other.\n\nBoth oral and written traditions are vital parts of the literary heritage of humanity, each with its unique strengths and modes of expression. Studying literature often involves appreciating elements derived from both.\n\n(Suggested insertion: Examples of specific oral tales or proverbs from relevant cultural contexts (e.g., West African). A brief comparison table highlighting key differences between oral and written traditions in terms of transmission, authorship, stability, and performance.)",
