          "title": "2.2 Narrative Techniques: Foreshadowing, Flashback, Stream of Consciousness (Continued)",
          "image": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/12113866/pexels-photo-12113866.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
          "video": "",
          "content": "Besides foreshadowing, flashback, and stream of consciousness, authors employ other techniques to shape their narratives:\n\nSuspense:\n\nThe quality in a story or play that makes the reader or audience feel anxious, curious, or uncertain about what will happen next.\n\nPurpose: To keep the reader engaged, build tension, and heighten emotional involvement.\n\nCreated by: Hinting at danger, posing questions, delaying resolution of conflicts, creating dilemmas for characters.\n\nPacing:\n\nThe speed at which the story unfolds. Authors control pacing by varying the amount of detail, the length of scenes, the use of dialogue versus description, and the frequency of action or reflection.\n\nPurpose: Can create excitement (fast pacing) or allow for reflection and character development (slower pacing). Can vary within a single work.\n\nDialogue:\n\nConversation between two or more characters.\n\nPurpose: Reveals character (personality, motivations, relationships), advances the plot, provides exposition (background information), creates realism, establishes tone. (More detail in Ch 3 on Drama).\n\nNarration (Point of View - Review from Ch 1.4): The perspective from which the story is told (First person, Third-person limited, Third-person omniscient, Third-person objective). The choice of narrator profoundly affects what information is revealed and how the reader perceives events and characters.\n\nJuxtaposition: Placing two or more ideas, characters, settings, or actions side-by-side for the purpose of comparison, contrast, or to create a particular effect.\n\nIn Medias Res (\"In the midst of things\"): Beginning a story in the middle of the action, rather than at the chronological beginning. Background information is often revealed later through flashbacks or dialogue.\n\nPurpose: Can create immediate interest and suspense.\n\nUnderstanding these techniques helps readers analyze how authors construct their narratives and achieve specific artistic or thematic goals.\n\n(Suggested insertion: Brief examples or scenarios illustrating suspense, pacing, and in medias res.)",
