          "title": "2.3 Criticism Approaches: Formalist, Historical, Marxist, Feminist, Postcolonial (Overview)",
          "image": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/7334191/pexels-photo-7334191.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
          "video": "",
          "content": "Literary Criticism (or Literary Theory): The study, discussion, evaluation, and interpretation of literature. Different critical approaches provide different lenses or frameworks through which to analyze and understand literary texts. Each approach focuses on different aspects of the text, its context, or its relationship with the reader.\n(This is a brief overview of some major schools; each is a complex field).\n\nFormalism (or New Criticism):\n\nFocus: Primarily on the literary text itself – its intrinsic features and form, independent of author's intention, reader's response, or historical/social context.\n\nKey Aspects Analyzed: Language (diction, syntax), structure, imagery, symbolism, figures of speech, literary devices, paradox, irony, ambiguity, tension, unity, and how these elements work together to create meaning and aesthetic effect within the text (\"close reading\").\n\nAims for an objective analysis of the work as a self-contained artistic object.\n\nHistorical/Biographical Criticism:\n\nFocus: Examines the literary work in relation to its historical, social, cultural, and intellectual context, as well as the life and times of the author.\n\nKey Aspects Analyzed: How historical events, social conditions, cultural values, philosophical ideas, and the author's personal experiences and intentions may have influenced the creation and meaning of the work. How the work reflects or responds to its historical period.\n\nUses historical documents, biographical information, and cultural studies to illuminate the text.\n\nMarxist Criticism:\n\nFocus: Based on the theories of Karl Marx; examines literature through the lens of social class, economic systems (especially capitalism), power structures, ideology, and the struggles between different social classes.\n\nKey Aspects Analyzed: How the work reflects or critiques class conflict, economic exploitation, alienation, materialism, and power dynamics. Representation of working class vs. ruling class. How economic factors influence characters and plot. Role of ideology in shaping consciousness.\n\nFeminist Criticism:\n\nFocus: Examines how literature portrays women, represents gender roles, challenges or reinforces patriarchal (male-dominated) assumptions and power structures, and explores female experiences and perspectives.\n\nKey Aspects Analyzed: Representation of female characters (stereotypes vs. complex portrayals), power dynamics between genders, societal expectations of women, experiences of oppression or resistance, female authorship, and the construction of gender in literature. Aims to recover and revalue women's voices and experiences.\n\nPostcolonial Criticism:\n\nFocus: Analyzes literature produced in countries that were formerly European colonies (e.g., in Africa, Asia, Caribbean) or literature that deals with the experience and effects of colonialism and imperialism.\n\nKey Aspects Analyzed: Themes of cultural identity, hybridity, race, power, oppression, resistance, nationalism, language, representation of colonizer and colonized, the legacy of colonialism, and the struggle for decolonization and self-representation. Explores how literature can challenge or perpetuate colonial ideologies.\n\nOther Critical Approaches (Brief Mention):\n\nReader-Response Criticism: Focuses on the reader's experience and interaction with the text in creating meaning.\n\nPsychoanalytic Criticism: Applies psychological theories (e.g., Freud, Jung) to analyze characters, authors, or the text itself.\n\nStructuralism and Post-structuralism/Deconstruction: Focus on underlying structures of language and meaning, or deconstruct texts to reveal multiple, often contradictory, meanings and the instability of language.\n\nQueer Theory, Ecocriticism, Disability Studies, etc.\n\nDifferent critical approaches are not necessarily mutually exclusive; they can offer complementary insights into a literary work. Understanding these approaches broadens the ways we can interpret and engage with literature.\n\n(Suggested insertion: A table briefly summarizing each critical approach (Formalist, Historical, Marxist, Feminist, Postcolonial) with its main focus and key questions it might ask about a text.)",
