          "content": "This section would focus on drawing comparisons between the plays studied and preparing for essay questions.\n\nComparative Study:\n\nThemes: Compare how themes like tradition vs. modernity, colonialism, the role of women, power, duty, or cultural identity are explored in two or more of the plays (e.g., Tradition vs. Modernity in The Lion and the Jewel vs. Death and the King's Horseman; Role of Women in Anowa vs. The Lion and the Jewel).\n\nCharacters: Compare and contrast key characters (e.g., protagonists, antagonists, female characters) across the plays in terms of their motivations, flaws, development, and roles.\n\nDramatic Techniques: Compare the playwrights' use of language, structure, symbolism, comedy/tragedy, or incorporation of cultural elements.\n\nContext: Discuss how the specific cultural, historical, or social contexts of each play shape its meaning and impact.\n\nTypical Critical Essay Questions for Drama:\n\nTheme-based: \"Discuss the theme of X in [Play Title].\" \"How does [Playwright] explore the conflict between A and B in [Play Title]?\"\n\nCharacter-based: \"Analyze the character of [Character Name] and their significance in [Play Title].\" \"To what extent is [Character Name] a tragic hero?\"\n\nTechnique-based: \"Examine the use of dramatic irony (or symbolism, or language) in [Play Title] and its contribution to the play's overall effect.\"\n\nContext-based: \"Discuss how the cultural/historical setting influences the events and themes of [Play Title].\"\n\nComparative: (As above).\n\n\"To what extent do you agree...\" type questions requiring a balanced argument.\n\nSkills for Writing Drama Essays:\n\nFormulating a clear thesis statement.\n\nStructuring the essay logically (introduction, body paragraphs with topic sentences, conclusion).\n\nUsing specific textual evidence (quotations, references to scenes/actions) to support points.\n\nAnalyzing dramatic techniques and their effects.\n\nShowing understanding of character, plot, theme, and context.\n\nUsing appropriate literary terminology.\n\nWriting in a clear, coherent, and formal academic style.\n\n(Suggested insertion: Example comparative essay prompts. A template or checklist for structuring a critical essay on a play. Guidance on how to select and integrate quotations effectively.)",
