          "title": "5.4 Figurative Language: Metaphor, Simile, Personification (Revisited for Poetic Emphasis)",
          "image": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/17577228/pexels-photo-17577228.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
          "video": "",
          "content": "(These were introduced in Ch 2.1 as general literary devices; here their specific power and prevalence in poetry are emphasized.)\nFigurative language allows poets to express ideas and emotions in fresh, vivid, and imaginative ways, moving beyond literal meaning.\n\nMetaphor: A direct comparison between two unlike things, stating or implying that one thing is another, without using \"like\" or \"as.\" It creates a strong identification.\n\nTenor: The subject being described.\n\nVehicle: The thing to which the subject is compared.\n\nExample: \"Hope is the thing with feathers / That perches in the soul\" (Emily Dickinson). (Tenor: Hope, Vehicle: a bird).\n\nImplied Metaphor: Comparison is suggested rather than stated directly.\n\nSimile: An explicit comparison between two unlike things, using \"like,\" \"as,\" \"than,\" or \"resembles.\"\n\nExample: \"My love is <u>like</u> a red, red rose\" (Robert Burns). \"The warrior fought <u>as</u> fiercely <u>as</u> a lion.\"\n\nPersonification: Attributing human qualities, emotions, or actions to inanimate objects, animals, or abstract ideas.\n\nExample: \"The\n\nExample: \"The wind whispered through the willows.\" \"The flowers danced in the breeze.\" \"Justice is blind.\"\nOther Important Figures of Speech in Poetry:\n\nApostrophe: Addressing an absent person, an abstract idea, or an inanimate object as if it were present and capable of understanding.\n\nExample: \"O wild West Wind, thou breath of Autumn's being...\" (Shelley). \"Twinkle, twinkle, little star, how I wonder what you are.\"\n\nHyperbole: (Exaggeration for effect).\n\nUnderstatement/Litotes: (Minimizing for effect).\n\nParadox/Oxymoron: (Contradictory statements/terms).\n\nSynecdoche: A part is used to represent the whole, or the whole for a part.\n\nExample: \"All hands on deck\" (hands for sailors). \"Wheels\" (for a car).\n\nMetonymy: Substituting the name of an attribute or adjunct for that of the thing meant.\n\nExample: \"The Crown\" (for the monarchy). \"The Pen is mightier than the Sword\" (writing for diplomacy/reason, weapon for warfare).\n\nAllusion: (Indirect reference).\n\nUnderstanding these figures helps to unlock the layers of meaning and appreciate the artistry in poetry.\n\n(Suggested insertion: Clear poetic examples for metaphor, simile, personification, apostrophe, synecdoche, and metonymy. Focus on how these devices enrich the poem's meaning and imagery.)",
