          "content": "Author: O. Henry (William Sydney Porter - American short story writer known for surprise endings).\n\nSetting: Greenwich Village, New York City, in the early 20th century, during an autumn/winter pneumonia epidemic.\n\nBrief Plot Summary: Two young female artists, Sue and Johnsy (Joanna), live together. Johnsy falls seriously ill with pneumonia and loses her will to live. She watches the leaves falling from an ivy vine outside her window and decides she will die when the last leaf falls. An old, unsuccessful painter named Behrman, who lives downstairs and has always dreamed of painting a masterpiece, scoffs at Johnsy's fancy. As the days pass and the leaves fall, Johnsy's condition worsens. One night, during a fierce storm, only one leaf remains on the vine. Sue expects it to fall by morning, but it is still there. Seeing the tenacious leaf, Johnsy regains her will to live and starts to recover. Later, Sue learns that Behrman died of pneumonia contracted after being out in the storm. He had painted the last leaf on the wall during the night, his masterpiece, which saved Johnsy's life.\n\nThemes:\n\nHope and Despair: Johnsy's despair versus the hope inspired by the leaf.\n\nSelf-Sacrifice and Love: Behrman's ultimate sacrifice for Johnsy. The love and care between Sue and Johnsy.\n\nThe Power of Art: Art (Behrman's painted leaf) literally saves a life and becomes his masterpiece.\n\nAppearance vs. Reality: The painted leaf appears real and gives hope.\n\nThe Will to Live.\n\nFriendship and Compassion.\n\nCharacter Study:\n\nJohnsy (Joanna): Young artist, ill and pessimistic, her fate tied to the falling leaves.\n\nSue: Johnsy's devoted friend and roommate, caring and practical.\n\nBehrman: Old, grumpy, seemingly failed artist, but ultimately selfless and heroic. His character is revealed through his final act.\n\nNarrative Techniques and Style:\n\nThird-person limited point of view (mostly from Sue's perspective).\n\nSituational Irony: Behrman, who scoffs at Johnsy's fanciful idea, ends up creating the ultimate \"fancy\" (a painted leaf) that saves her, at the cost of his own life. The masterpiece he longed to paint was not a grand canvas but a single leaf.\n\nSymbolism: The ivy leaves (Johnsy's declining hope/life), the last leaf (tenacity, hope, Behrman's sacrifice, true art). The storm.\n\nSentimental and poignant tone.\n\nSurprise ending (characteristic of O. Henry).\n\nCritical Approaches:\n\nFormalist: Focus on the plot structure, irony, symbolism, and how elements contribute to the theme of sacrifice.\n\nEthical/Moral: The lessons about selflessness and the value of life.\n\n(Full analysis would involve: Detailed discussion of the plot twist and its impact, analysis of Behrman's character transformation (or revelation), in-depth exploration of the symbolism of the last leaf, and examination of O. Henry's use of irony and sentimentality.)",
