          "content": "Playwright: William Shakespeare (English, Elizabethan/Jacobean era).\n\nGenre: Often classified as a comedy (ends with marriages), but contains significant tragic and problematic elements, especially concerning Shylock.\n\nSetting: Venice (a bustling commercial city-state) and Belmont (a more idyllic, romantic setting), Italy, 16th century.\n\nBrief Plot Summary: Bassanio needs money to woo the wealthy heiress Portia. His friend Antonio, a merchant, borrows money for him from Shylock, a Jewish moneylender. Antonio, confident his ships will return, agrees to a bond: if he defaults, Shylock can take a pound of his flesh. Portia's father has decreed suitors must choose correctly from three caskets (gold, silver, lead) to win her. Bassanio chooses correctly. Meanwhile, Antonio's ships are lost, and he defaults. Shylock, embittered by past mistreatment and his daughter Jessica's elopement with a Christian (Lorenzo), insists on the lethal bond. Portia, disguised as a young male lawyer, saves Antonio in a dramatic trial scene by cleverly interpreting the bond (no blood can be shed). Shylock is defeated and forced to convert to Christianity. The play ends with happy marriages but leaves lingering questions about justice and mercy.\n\nThemes:\n\nJustice vs. Mercy: Central to the trial scene and the treatment of Shylock.\n\nLove and Friendship: Various forms (romantic, platonic).\n\nPrejudice and Intolerance (Anti-Semitism): The play's portrayal of Shylock and the anti-Jewish attitudes of the Christian characters is highly controversial and problematic for modern audiences.\n\nWealth, Commerce, and Usury.\n\nAppearance vs. Reality: The caskets, disguises.\n\nThe Law and its Interpretation.\n\nRevenge.\n\nKey Characters:\n\nAntonio: The merchant of Venice. Melancholy, loyal to Bassanio, anti-Semitic.\n\nBassanio: Antonio's friend, suitor to Portia.\n\nPortia: Wealthy, intelligent, resourceful heiress of Belmont. Disguises herself as lawyer Balthazar.\n\nShylock: Jewish moneylender. A complex and controversial character: victim of prejudice, vengeful, but also articulate in his grievances (\"Hath not a Jew eyes?\").\n\nNerissa: Portia's waiting-woman and confidante.\n\nGratiano, Lorenzo, Salanio, Salerio: Friends of Antonio and Bassanio.\n\nJessica: Shylock's daughter, elopes with Lorenzo and converts to Christianity.\n\nDramatic Techniques:\n\nBlank verse (iambic pentameter) and prose.\n\nMultiple interwoven plots (casket plot, bond plot, elopement plot).\n\nDramatic irony, suspense.\n\nSoliloquies.\n\nRich imagery and wordplay.\n\nDisguise.\n\nHistorical Context: Elizabethan England – societal attitudes towards Jews, commerce in Venice, legal systems. Requires careful contextualization to understand its original reception vs. modern interpretations.\n\n(Full analysis: Detailed character studies of Portia and Shylock. Scene analysis of the trial scene. Thematic discussion focusing on justice/mercy and prejudice, with textual evidence. Analysis of Shakespeare's language and dramatic structure. Discussion of historical context and different critical interpretations (especially regarding Shylock).)",
          "mcq": {
            "question": "What is the central ethical dilemma explored in the trial scene of 'The Merchant of Venice'?",
            "options": [
              "Love vs. Friendship",
              "Appearance vs. Reality",
              "Justice vs. Mercy",
              "Wealth vs. Poverty"
            ],
            "correctAnswer": "Justice vs. Mercy",
            "explanation": "The trial scene pivots on the conflict between Shylock's demand for strict, literal justice (the bond) and Portia's appeal for mercy as a higher virtue."
          }
