          "title": "11.2 “A Doll’s House” by Henrik Ibsen (Feminism, Realism, Social Critique)",
          "image": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/15309299/pexels-photo-15309299.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
          "video": "",
          "content": "Playwright: Henrik Ibsen (Norwegian, \"father of modern drama\").\n\nGenre: Realist drama, problem play.\n\nSetting: The Helmer household in Norway, late 19th century (Christmas time).\n\nBrief Plot Summary: Nora Helmer appears to be a playful, childlike wife to her bank manager husband, Torvald, who treats her condescendingly. Years earlier, Nora secretly and illegally borrowed money to finance a trip to Italy to save Torvald's life, forging her father's signature. Krogstad, a disgraced bank employee from whom Nora borrowed the money, threatens to expose her secret if she doesn't prevent Torvald from firing him. As Nora tries to manage the situation, her illusions about her marriage and her own identity begin to crumble. When Torvald learns the truth, his reaction is selfish and concerned only with his own reputation. Though Krogstad later relents, Nora realizes the hollowness of her marriage and her life as a \"doll\" in a \"doll's house.\" In a shocking (for its time) final act, she decides to leave Torvald and her children to find her own identity and educate herself.\n\nThemes:\n\nThe Role and Status of Women in 19th Century Society (Feminist Critique): Examines the restrictive social norms, limited opportunities, and societal expectations placed on women, particularly within marriage. Nora's journey to self-awareness and independence.\n\nMarriage and Individual Identity: Critiques superficial, patriarchal marriages based on societal conventions rather than true partnership and equality. The need for individual self-discovery.\n\nAppearance vs. Reality: The seemingly happy Helmer marriage conceals lies, deception, and a lack of genuine understanding.\n\nDeception and its Consequences.\n\nMoney, Debt, and Social Reputation.\n\nIndividual Freedom and Responsibility.\n\nThe \"Sins of the Fathers\" (and mothers) - heredity and environment.\n\nKey Characters:\n\nNora Helmer: The protagonist. Initially appears frivolous and submissive but undergoes a profound transformation to become a strong, independent woman seeking self-realization.\n\nTorvald Helmer: Nora's husband. Pompous, conventional, controlling, obsessed with appearances and reputation. Treats Nora as a possession or child.\n\nNils Krogstad: Bank clerk, a morally ambiguous character driven by desperation to protect his reputation and provide for his children.\n\nMrs. Kristine Linde: Nora's old school friend. Practical, independent, has faced hardship. Acts as a confidante and catalyst for Nora.\n\nDr. Rank: Family friend, secretly in love with Nora, dying. Represents a more cynical view of life.\n\nDramatic Techniques (Realism):\n\nFocus on ordinary, middle-class characters and contemporary social problems.\n\nRealistic dialogue and setting.\n\nWell-made play structure (exposition, rising action, climax, resolution) but with a radical, open-ended resolution for its time.\n\nPsychological depth in characterization.\n\nUse of symbolism (e.g., the Christmas tree, macaroons, Nora's tarantella dance, the slammed door at the end).\n\nRetrospective exposition (revealing past events gradually).\n\n(Full analysis: Detailed study of Nora's character development. Analysis of Torvald as a representative of patriarchal attitudes. Discussion of the themes of marriage, gender roles, and individual freedom, with specific examples from the play. Examination of Ibsen's use of realism, symbolism, and the significance of the play's ending. Consideration of its historical impact.)",
