          "title": "11.4 Comparative Study of Classical vs. Modern Drama Techniques",
          "image": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/16354547/pexels-photo-16354547.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
          "video": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=R45ry1A4nyY",
          "content": "This section would involve comparing and contrasting the dramatic conventions and techniques of earlier forms of drama (e.g., Greek tragedy, Shakespearean drama) with those of modern drama (e.g., Realism like Ibsen, Absurdism like Beckett).\nPoints of Comparison:\n\nPlot Structure:\n\nClassical/Shakespearean: Often well-defined plot (Freytag's Pyramid), clear beginning-middle-end, focus on causality and resolution.\n\nModern (Realism): Still often structured, but focus on psychological realism and social issues; resolution might be less definitive.\n\nModern (Absurdism): Often rejects traditional plot; circular, repetitive, or fragmented structures; lack of clear resolution.\n\nCharacterization:\n\nClassical/Shakespearean: Often heroic or noble protagonists (tragedy), archetypal characters, clear motivations (though complex). Use of soliloquies for inner thought.\n\nModern (Realism): Focus on psychologically complex, ordinary individuals, often shaped by social environment.\n\nModern (Absurdism): Characters may be ill-defined, lack clear motivation, represent types or aspects of human condition rather than fully rounded individuals.\n\nDialogue:\n\nClassical/Shakespearean: Often poetic (blank verse, rhymed couplets), elevated language, rhetorical speeches, asides, soliloquies.\n\nModern (Realism): Aims for naturalistic, everyday speech patterns. Subtext important.\n\nModern (Absurdism): Dialogue can be fragmented, nonsensical, repetitive, clichéd, reflecting failure of communication.\n\nSetting and Staging:\n\nClassical/Shakespearean: Minimalist sets (Elizabethan), use of conventions (e.g., a \"place\" represented by words).\n\nModern (Realism): Detailed, realistic sets and props to create illusion of reality (\"box set\").\n\nModern (Absurdism): Often sparse, symbolic, or non-realistic settings.\n\nThemes:\n\nClassical/Shakespearean: Often grand themes (fate, destiny, ambition, justice, love, order vs. chaos).\n\nModern (Realism): Focus on social problems, individual psychology, domestic issues, critiques of societal norms.\n\nModern (Absurdism): Existential themes (meaninglessness, alienation, anxiety, failure of communication).\n\nAudience Relationship:\n\nClassical/Shakespearean: Direct address (prologues, epilogues, asides) common. Audience aware of theatricality.\n\nModern (Realism): Aims for \"fourth wall\" illusion, audience as observer of \"real life.\"\n\nModern (Absurdism): Often challenges or alienates audience, breaks illusion, provokes discomfort or thought.\n\nUse of Chorus/Narrator: Common in Greek tragedy, less so in Shakespeare (though some characters have choric functions), largely absent in Realism, sometimes reappears in modified forms in later modern/postmodern drama.\n\n(Suggested insertion: A comparative table highlighting key differences between, for example, Shakespearean Tragedy, Ibsenite Realism, and Beckettian Absurdism across categories like Plot, Character, Dialogue, Setting, Themes, and Audience Relationship. Use specific examples from plays discussed or other well-known plays to illustrate points.)",
