          "title": "12.1 “The Second Coming” by W. B. Yeats (Imagery, Symbolism, Historical Context)",
          "image": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/7334191/pexels-photo-7334191.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
          "video": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6ipXPb2xU",
          "content": "Poet: William Butler Yeats (Irish poet, Nobel Laureate). Written in 1919.\n\nOverview/Subject Matter: A powerful and prophetic poem reflecting a sense of crisis, chaos, and the breakdown of order in the post-World War I era. It envisions a cyclical view of history, where one era is ending and a new, perhaps terrifying, age is dawning.\n\nKey Themes:\n\nAnarchy, Chaos, and the Breakdown of Social/Moral Order (\"Things fall apart; the centre cannot hold\").\n\nLoss of Innocence and Traditional Values (\"The ceremony of innocence is drowned\").\n\nThe End of an Era (specifically the Christian era or Western civilization as Yeats perceived it).\n\nThe Coming of a New, Unsettling Age or Dispensation (the \"rough beast\").\n\nHistorical Cycles (Yeats's mystical theory of \"gyres\" - widening spiral of history).\n\nViolence and Spiritual Emptiness.\n\nPoetic Techniques and Imagery:\n\nPowerful, Apocalyptic Imagery: \"blood-dimmed tide,\" \"anarchy loosed,\" \"rough beast...slouches towards Bethlehem to be born.\"\n\nSymbolism:\n\nThe Falcon and the Falconer: Loss of control, breakdown of order.\n\nThe \"Gyre\": Yeats's symbol for historical cycles.\n\nThe \"Rough Beast\" / Sphinx-like figure: A terrifying new force or anti-Christ emerging.\n\nBethlehem: Ironic reference to the birthplace of Christ, suggesting a perverse or opposite kind Df second coming.\n\n\"Spiritus Mundi\": A collective soul or world memory from which images arise.\n\nStructure: Two stanzas. First describes current chaos, second a prophetic vision. Fairly regular iambic pentameter, but with variations and a strong, often harsh, rhythm. Some slant rhymes.\n\nTone: Urgent, prophetic, despairing, but also visionary.\n\nHistorical Context: Written shortly after World War I (1914-1918), the Russian Revolution (1917), and the Irish Easter Rising (1916). Reflects the widespread sense of crisis, disillusionment, and fear of societal collapse in Europe. Also influenced by Yeats's personal mystical and occult beliefs.\n\n(Full analysis: Line-by-line/stanza-by-stanza interpretation. Detailed analysis of key symbols (falcon, gyre, rough beast). Discussion of historical and biographical contexts. Examination of Yeats's use of imagery, rhythm, and prophetic tone.)",
