          "title": "12.2 “Do Not Go Gentle into That Good Night” by Dylan Thomas (Villanelle Form, Theme of Resistance)",
          "image": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/32503380/pexels-photo-32503380.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
          "video": "",
          "content": "Poet: Dylan Thomas (Welsh poet). Written in 1951 for his dying father.\n\nForm: A Villanelle – a complex and strict French poetic form consisting of 19 lines: five tercets (three-line stanzas) followed by a final quatrain (four-line stanza). It has only two rhyme sounds, and two lines are systematically repeated throughout the poem:\n\nLine 1 of the first tercet is repeated as the last line of the second and fourth tercets.\n\nLine 3 of the first tercet is repeated as the last line of the third and fifth tercets.\n\nThese two refrain lines appear as the final two lines of the concluding quatrain.\n\nRhyme scheme: A¹BA² abA¹ abA² abA¹ abA² abA¹A².\n\nOverview/Subject Matter: The poem is an impassioned plea from a son to his dying father to resist death and fight against \"the dying of the light.\" It explores different ways men face death and advocates for a fierce, defiant struggle against mortality.\n\nThemes:\n\nRage against Death / Resistance to Mortality: The central theme. Urging the father (and others) to fight against dying.\n\nLife and its Intensity: Different types of men (wise, good, wild, grave) are shown to have reasons to rage against death because their lives, though perhaps unfulfilled or imperfect, had meaning or potential.\n\nLight and Darkness as Metaphors: Light for life, consciousness, vitality; Night/Darkness for death, oblivion.\n\nThe Power of Emotion (Anger, Grief, Love).\n\nFather-Son Relationship.\n\nPoetic Techniques and Imagery:\n\nVillanelle Form: The repetition of the lines \"Do not go gentle into that good night\" and \"Rage, rage against the dying of the light\" creates a powerful, incantatory effect, emphasizing the poem's central message. The strict form provides a container for intense emotion.\n\nDirect Address (Apostrophe): To his father.\n\nStrong Verbs and Vivid Imagery: \"burn and rave,\" \"forked no lightning,\" \"frail deeds might have danced,\" \"blinding sight.\"\n\nMetaphors: \"Good night\" for death, \"dying of the light\" for loss of life/consciousness.\n\nContrast: Between \"gentle\" acceptance and \"rage\"; light and dark; life and death.\n\nTone: Urgent, passionate, defiant, pleading, but also tinged with grief and love.\n\nContext: Written during the final illness of Dylan Thomas's father. Personal and universal.\n\n(Full analysis: Detailed examination of how the villanelle form (repetition, rhyme) reinforces the poem's themes and emotional intensity. Analysis of the different types of men described in stanzas 2-5 and their reasons for \"raging.\" Interpretation of the key metaphors of light/darkness. Discussion of the poem's powerful tone.)",
          "mcq": {
            "question": "'Do Not Go Gentle into That Good Night' is written in which strict poetic form that relies on repeating lines?",
            "options": [
              "Sonnet",
              "Haiku",
              "Elegy",
              "Villanelle"
            ],
            "correctAnswer": "Villanelle",
            "explanation": "The poem is a villanelle, a 19-line form where two lines are repeated in a specific pattern, creating an urgent, incantatory effect that reinforces the theme of raging against death."
          }
