          "title": "13.1 Formalism/New Criticism: Focus on Textual Detail",
          "image": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/7334191/pexels-photo-7334191.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
          "video": "",
          "content": "Period of Dominance: Early to mid-20th century (especially in Anglo-American criticism).\n\nCore Principle: Focuses on the literary work as a self-contained, autonomous object, independent of the author's biography or intention (Intentional Fallacy), the reader's emotional response (Affective Fallacy), or its historical/social context. Meaning resides within the text itself.\n\nMethodology: Close Reading. Detailed analysis of the text's formal elements and how they interact to create a unified, complex meaning.\n\nKey Elements Analyzed:\n\nLanguage: Diction, syntax, figures of speech (metaphor, simile, irony, paradox, ambiguity), imagery, symbolism.\n\nStructure: Form, plot, organization of parts.\n\nSound devices (in poetry): Rhyme, rhythm, meter.\n\nInternal relationships, patterns, tensions, and resolutions within the text.\n\nAim is to demonstrate the \"organic unity\" of the work – how all parts contribute to a coherent whole.\n\nGoal: To provide an objective interpretation of the text based solely on its internal evidence.\n\nStrengths: Encourages careful, detailed attention to the text itself and the artistry of language. Provides a systematic way to analyze literary devices.\n\nCriticisms/Limitations: Can be seen as neglecting important historical, social, cultural, or authorial contexts that shape meaning. Accused of treating texts in a vacuum.",
