          "title": "13.2 Marxist Criticism: Economic and Social Class Analysis",
          "image": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/7334191/pexels-photo-7334191.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
          "video": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_AjNEoBizGk",
          "content": "Theoretical Basis: Based on the social, economic, and political theories of Karl Marx and Friedrich Engels.\n\nCore Principle: Views literature as a reflection or product of the socio-economic conditions and class struggles within a society. Literature is not produced in a vacuum but is shaped by material realities and ideologies.\n\nKey Concepts:\n\nBase and Superstructure: Economic base (means of production, relations of production) shapes the superstructure (culture, institutions, art, literature, ideology).\n\nClass Struggle: Conflict between the bourgeoisie (ruling class, owners of production) and the proletariat (working class).\n\nIdeology: Systems of beliefs and values that support the dominant class and often mask or justify exploitation.\n\nAlienation: The estrangement of workers from their labor, products, and themselves under capitalism.\n\nHegemony: Dominance of one class's ideology.\n\nMethodology: Analyzes texts to reveal:\n\nHow social class and economic factors are represented and influence characters, plot, and themes.\n\nPortrayal of class conflict, exploitation, poverty, and inequality.\n\nHow the work supports or critiques dominant ideologies or power structures.\n\nThe relationship between the text and the economic conditions of its production.\n\nThe author's class position and how it might influence the work.\n\nGoal: To understand literature in its socio-economic context and often to promote awareness of social injustice or advocate for change.",
