          "title": "13.3 Feminist and Gender Criticism: Representation of Gender Roles",
          "image": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/5198202/pexels-photo-5198202.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
          "video": "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=QYJTTGLNNIw",
          "content": "Feminist Criticism:\n\nCore Principle: Examines how literature portrays, perpetuates, or challenges patriarchal (male-dominated) assumptions, power structures, and the representation of women and gender roles. Aims to recover and value female voices, experiences, and literary traditions.\n\nKey Concepts:\n\nPatriarchy: Social system where men hold primary power and authority.\n\nGender vs. Sex: Sex as biological, gender as socially constructed roles and identities.\n\nStereotypes: Traditional, often limiting, representations of women (e.g., angel/monster, passive/emotional).\n\nThe Male Gaze: How literature (often written by men) can depict the world and women from a masculine perspective, objectifying women.\n\nSilencing and Marginalization: How women's voices and experiences have been excluded or devalued in literary canons.\n\nMethodology:\n\nAnalyzing the portrayal of female characters: Are they complex individuals or stereotypes? Do they have agency?\n\nExamining power dynamics between male and female characters.\n\nIdentifying how gender roles and societal expectations for women are depicted and critiqued (or reinforced).\n\nExploring themes related to women's experiences (e.g., marriage, motherhood, oppression, resistance, search for identity).\n\nRecovering and analyzing works by female authors (gynocriticism).\n\nChallenging traditional literary canons.\n\nGender Criticism (often encompassing Queer Theory):\n\nExpands on feminist criticism to explore the social construction of gender and sexuality more broadly.\n\nChallenges binary notions of gender (male/female) and sexuality (heterosexual/homosexual).\n\nExamines how literature represents diverse gender identities and sexual orientations, and how normative gender/sexual roles are constructed or deconstructed.\n\nLooks at issues of masculinity as well as femininity.",
